<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  WHAT A RUN LEARNED ABOUT ITS OWN SESSIONS — a child machine `ai_loop.scxml`
  invokes before it replaces one, so the session that comes next starts from
  something better than a fresh reading of the same files.

  ⚠⚠⚠ WHY THIS IS A DOCUMENT AND NOT A FUNCTION. The analysis has STEPS with
  different failure modes — the records may not be readable, the counting may
  find nothing worth carrying, the narrow question may come back unusable — and
  a caller has to be able to tell those apart. A function returning
  `Option<String>` collapses all three into "nothing came back". Written as a
  machine, each is a state somebody can read in the run's own journal.

  ⚠⚠ AND BECAUSE THE PARENT OWNS ITS LIFECYCLE. `<invoke>` means the loop that
  asked for the analysis also cancels it by leaving the state — measured in this
  tree by `a_document_here_can_invoke_a_child_machine_and_hear_what_it_answered`,
  which is what licensed this file to exist at all.

  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT WAS MEASURED BEFORE ANY OF THIS WAS DESIGNED, and it corrected the
  design twice. One real session of this repo, 759,676 tokens of accumulated
  context, opened and counted:

    tool RESULTS              753,645 bytes    (99%)
    the agent's own words       7,144 bytes    ( 1%)

      Read   457,492 bytes over  54 calls   of which 18% overlapping line ranges
      Bash   272,090 bytes over 618 calls   of which 44% is repeated commands
      Edit    23,181 bytes over 140 calls

  ⚠⚠⚠ THE FIRST DESIGN AIMED AT THE 1%. It was going to write a better handover
  note — the agent's own summary of what it had learned — which is a lever over
  seven thousand bytes of seven hundred and fifty thousand.

  ⚠⚠⚠ THE SECOND AIMED AT A NUMBER THAT WAS WRONG. Counting Reads BY FILE PATH
  said "45% of the context is re-reading", and opening one file's history showed
  eleven reads at eleven DIFFERENT offsets — a large file read in pieces, which
  is correct behaviour. By line range the real overlap is 18%, and most of that
  is unavoidable boundary overlap between adjacent windows.

  What survived both corrections is the one that was measured rather than
  assumed: 618 Bash calls, 44% of them repeats, and the top five are all one
  thing —

     84x  grep for failures in the build log
     74x  tail the background build's output
     50x  grep for a failing test
     27x  tail the newest build log
     25x  grep for the test result

  Two hundred and sixty calls asking "is the build finished yet?", each answer
  kept in the context for ever, and all but the last of them already stale.

  ⚠⚠ SO THE THING WORTH CARRYING IS NOT A SUMMARY OF THE WORK. It is the small
  set of facts that would have stopped the next session doing the same thing —
  and POLLING is the one this measurement can actually see.

  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  ⚠⚠⚠ WHO DECIDES WHAT. The driver COUNTS and this document DECIDES, which is
  the arrangement `ai_loop.scxml` already uses for a judged dialog and for the
  same reason: a threshold in the driver is a policy nobody can read, and a
  count in the document is arithmetic nobody can do here.

    the driver   opens the records, counts commands and line ranges, and
                 publishes totals as event data
    this file    when to look, how many repeats are too many, what to ask,
                 where the answer may be written
    a model      one line per candidate, and nothing else — it never chooses
                 WHICH candidates there are

  ⚠ The last of those is the safety property. A model that picked the candidates
  would be summarising a session, which is the thing measured above as a 1%
  lever and an unbounded prompt.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0"
       name="context_review"
       datamodel="ecmascript"
       initial="reading">

  <datamodel>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ HOW MANY TIMES A COMMAND MUST HAVE BEEN RUN BEFORE IT COUNTS AS A
      HABIT. Authored, because it is the whole judgement: two is "it happened
      again", and a hundred is "nothing will ever be reported".

      ⚠ It is deliberately NOT tuned. The measurement above found repeats at 84,
      74, 50, 27 and 25 — an order of magnitude above any plausible threshold —
      so the number does not need to be right to be useful, and pretending to
      have derived it would be the folklore this workspace keeps finding.
    -->
    <data id="repeat_limit" expr="8"/>

    <!--
      HOW MANY SESSIONS BACK TO LOOK. A run replaces its session on every
      adopted reflection, so this is a count of ENDED sessions, and each one is
      a whole transcript the driver reads.

      ⚠⚠ A SESSION IS NOT A UNIT OF ANYTHING EITHER — the same warning
      `ai_loop.scxml` carries about `max_turns`. Measured across this run's own
      sessions: one ended after 170 tool calls and another after 650. So three
      sessions is three transcripts, not three of anything comparable.
    -->
    <data id="look_back" expr="3"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHERE THE ANSWER MAY BE WRITTEN, and its default writes nowhere.

      An empty path is a review that COUNTS and REPORTS and changes no file,
      which is the honest default for a machine whose output is advice about
      somebody else's work. Setting it is the author saying *I have read what
      this writes and I want it kept*.

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT IS NOT `CLAUDE.md` AND MUST NOT BECOME IT BY ACCIDENT. That file is
      instructions a person depends on; a loop rewriting it every few sessions
      drifts somewhere nobody is watching, and the drift then drives the next
      session. The owner's ruling: the loop writes its own file, and promoting
      anything out of it into CLAUDE.md is a separate act with a person in it.
      The same shape as promoting a daemon binary, and for the same reason.
    -->
    <data id="write_into" expr="''"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHERE THE MEASUREMENT LANDS, and unlike `write_into` its default is a
      REAL PATH. The two are different kinds of thing and the difference is the
      whole reason this is a second knob rather than a reuse of the first.

      `write_into` holds ADVICE — a line a model wrote about somebody else's
      work — and its honest default is nowhere, for the reason written above it.
      This holds what was COUNTED: how many records were opened, how many calls
      they issued, how many were re-issued. That is not an opinion about anyone,
      it is what this run's own sessions did, and there is exactly one thing it
      is for.

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT IS FOR COMPARING A REVIEW WITH THE ONE BEFORE IT. A review that
      counts and discards can report *three habits* for ever and nobody can ask
      whether that is better or worse than last time — which makes «improve the
      loop's own context» a sentence with no measurement under it. The counts
      are cheap, the sessions they describe are CLOSED and their panes gone, and
      nothing can go back and count again. So the default keeps them.

      ⚠⚠ AND IT IS WRITTEN ON EVERY ENDING, not only on `carried`. The exit a
      healthy run takes is `nothing` — no habit met the limit — and that is
      precisely the reading a later comparison needs. Keeping the record only
      when something was wrong would retain the bad reviews and discard the
      evidence that anything got better.

      ⚠ A bare name is resolved by the driver against the daemon's state
      directory; an absolute path is used as given. That is the same kind of
      resolution `spend::record_of` already does for an agent's own record, and
      it is mechanism rather than policy: this document decides WHETHER to keep
      the counts and what to call them, not where a machine puts its state.

      ⚠ Empty turns it off, as `write_into` does. A caller who has read what
      this writes and does not want it says so.
    -->
    <data id="ledger_into" expr="'context-review.jsonl'"/>

    <!--
      WHAT A SECOND AGENT IS ASKED ABOUT ONE CANDIDATE — authored, because it is
      said to somebody's agent, and the driver must never be the thing that
      writes words into a prompt.

      ⚠ It asks for a HABIT TO BREAK rather than for a summary: the candidate is
      already chosen by counting, so the only thing left for a model to do is
      say what the next session should do instead, in one line.
    -->
    <data id="ask"
          expr="'A session repeated this command many times over one piece of work. In ONE line, say what the next session should do instead. If nothing would have helped, answer exactly: NOTHING TO CARRY'"/>

    <!-- The label a usable answer opens its line with, read back the way
         `ai_loop.scxml` reads a reflection's.

         ⚠⚠ NOTHING CONSULTS IT YET — see `asking` below. The second agent it
         describes is not wired, so this is a decision authored ahead of its
         mechanism; it is named here so a reader meets that fact at the
         declaration rather than inferring it from a grep. -->
    <data id="carry_marker" expr="'INSTEAD:'"/>

    <!-- ── below the line: what the driver publishes, not what an author writes ── -->

    <!-- How many ended sessions the driver actually found records for. -->
    <data id="records" expr="0"/>
    <!-- How many candidates met `repeat_limit`. -->
    <data id="habits" expr="0"/>
    <!-- What is being handed back to the parent. -->
    <data id="carry" expr="''"/>
  </datamodel>

  <!--
    Open the ended sessions' records. The driver knows which they are because the
    run minted an identity for each session it started.

    ⚠⚠ A RECORD THAT CANNOT BE READ IS THE ORDINARY CASE, not an error: an agent
    whose transcript is off writes none, and that is exactly what a nested agent
    marker does. So `read.none` is an EXIT rather than a failure — the review
    reports that it had nothing to look at, and the loop carries on unchanged.
  -->
  <state id="reading">
    <onentry>
      <send event="read.begin"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="read.done" cond="_event.data.records" target="counting">
      <assign location="records" expr="_event.data.records"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="read.none" target="nothing"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND AN ERROR OF THIS DOCUMENT'S OWN ENDS THE REVIEW RATHER THAN
      VANISHING — register item 505, and here the reachable cause is one line up.

      W3C SCXML 3.12.2 ignores an `error.*` nothing matches, and W3C 3.8 abandons
      the rest of the block that raised it. The guard above reads
      `_event.data.records`, so a `read.done` the driver raises WITHOUT data asks
      Lua to index nil: the guard cannot be evaluated, this transition is never
      taken, and until this edge existed the machine sat in `reading` for ever
      while the loop waited on a review that had already failed.

      `failed` is this document's own word for it, exactly as the `fail` edge
      above uses it, and the parent treats a failed review as no carry, which is
      the safe direction: a habit nobody found beats a habit invented by a
      half-executed block.

      ⚠⚠ FOUR COPIES HERE AND ONE IN `ai_loop.scxml`, and the difference is the
      shape rather than a preference: this document is FLAT, so there is no
      ancestor to hold the rule, and wrapping the four states in one would add a
      state to the type the driver matches exhaustively. What keeps the copies
      honest is the ratchet in `document.rs`, which requires EVERY state that
      runs executable content to be covered by an edge of its own or an
      ancestor's, so a fifth state cannot be added without one.
    -->
    <transition event="error.execution" target="failed"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    Count what was done more than once.

    ⚠⚠⚠ COUNT ACTS, NOT NAMES. The driver counts a COMMAND's repeats and a
    read's LINE RANGES, and the difference is not pedantry: counting reads by
    file path said 45% of a session was re-reading, and the same session counted
    by line range said 18%, because a large file read in eleven pieces is eleven
    reads of one path and nothing repeated at all. **An aggregate is not a
    finding until somebody opens one of its members.**
  -->
  <state id="counting">
    <onentry>
      <send event="count.begin"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="count.done" cond="_event.data.habits" target="asking">
      <assign location="habits" expr="_event.data.habits"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="count.none" target="nothing"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <!-- The region rule of a flat document, state by state. See `reading`. -->
    <transition event="error.execution" target="failed"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    Ask a second agent for one line per habit — and only per habit. The list it
    is given was decided by arithmetic before it was ever asked.

    ⚠ An answer that carries no `carry_marker` is `ask.none`, for the reason a
    judged dialog answers `false` on silence: the failure of this mechanism must
    be its own absence, never a line invented to fill the slot.

    ⚠⚠⚠ AND THAT RULE IS AUTHORED AHEAD OF ITS MECHANISM, WHICH THIS SAID AS
    THOUGH IT WERE ALREADY IN FORCE. No second agent is wired: the driver answers
    `ask.none` UNCONDITIONALLY here, so `ask.done` is unreachable and nothing —
    not this document, not `review.rs` — ever reads `carry_marker`. Measured
    2026-08-17 over every `<data>` in this crate's eight documents, counting a
    USE as an `expr`/`cond`/`location`/`<param>` or a Rust reader rather than a
    mention in prose: it is the only one of sixty-one that nothing consults.

    ⚠⚠ IT STAYS RATHER THAN GOING, and the distinction is the owner's rule.
    Decisions belong in the `.scxml`, so a marker authored before the code that
    needs it is a decision waiting for a mechanism, not dead weight — what was
    wrong was a sentence that let a reader believe the mechanism had arrived.
    `review.rs` says the same thing at its own site; these two now agree.
  -->
  <state id="asking">
    <onentry>
      <send event="ask.begin"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="ask.done" target="writing">
      <assign location="carry" expr="_event.data.carry"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="ask.none" target="nothing"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <!-- The region rule of a flat document, state by state. See `reading`. -->
    <transition event="error.execution" target="failed"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    Keep it where the author said, if the author said anywhere.

    ⚠ `write_into` is empty by default, so the ordinary review REPORTS and writes
    nothing. The parent still hears `carry` either way — a review that changed no
    file has still learned something, and hiding that behind an unset path would
    make the default look like a failure.
  -->
  <state id="writing">
    <onentry>
      <send event="write.begin"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="write.done" target="carried"/>
    <transition event="write.skipped" target="carried"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <!-- The region rule of a flat document, state by state. See `reading`. -->
    <transition event="error.execution" target="failed"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠ TWO ENDINGS THAT ARE BOTH SUCCESS, and they are separate because the
    parent may want to know which. `carried` means the next session is being
    handed something; `nothing` means the review ran and found no habit worth
    naming — which is the answer a healthy run should give most of the time.
  -->
  <final id="carried">
    <donedata>
      <param name="carry" expr="carry"/>
      <param name="habits" expr="habits"/>
    </donedata>
  </final>

  <final id="nothing">
    <donedata>
      <param name="carry" expr="''"/>
      <param name="habits" expr="0"/>
    </donedata>
  </final>

  <final id="failed"/>
</scxml>
